watercards=[('A • Rooted-plant model','Soil water enters roots, then travels inside the stem to leaves. Some water leaves as vapour.'),('B • Setup','Two similar cut white-flower shoots: one in red-coloured water, one in plain water. Neither has roots.'),('C • After 2 hours','Constructed observation: red lines appear inside the dye shoot’s adult-cut stem section.'),('D • After 24 hours','Constructed observation: some petals show red colour. Exact timing varies in real plants.'),('E • Plain-water comparison','Constructed observation: no red dye colour in the control stem or petals.'),('F • Water-level check','A separate open cup without a plant also loses some water. Evaporation is possible.')]
d['slides']=[slide('Opening',2,'How can water reach a leaf above the soil?',['Trace a route.','Use a marker to test part of it.'],'Distinguish the rooted-plant explanation from the rootless demonstration. Neither pupils nor the lesson need to wait for colour to develop.'),slide('Retrieval',3,'Retrieve the connected parts',['1. Which part takes in soil water?','2. Which part connects roots and leaves?','3. Does water usually enter as solid soil?'],'1 roots. 2 stem. 3 no; roots absorb water and dissolved mineral nutrients, not chunks of soil.'),slide('I do',7,'I trace the rooted-plant route',['Soil → roots → stem → leaves.','Water moves through internal pathways.'],'Think aloud: “I begin with water in soil, enter a root and follow xylem through the stem to a leaf. Some water evaporates and leaves as vapour.” Evaporation from leaves helps pull water upward; a straw-sucking mouth is not the mechanism.',visual=wholewater),slide('I do',0,'I mark water in a cut shoot',['Colour helps us follow movement.','A plain-water shoot is the comparison.','This setup has no roots.'],'Shares I do time. Think aloud: “I can investigate movement in this shoot, but I cannot claim to have shown root uptake because there are no roots.” Food colouring marks some pathways; it is not normal plant food.',visual=cutstem()),slide('I do',0,'I inspect the interior evidence',['Colour appears in internal paths.','Not all tissue is coloured equally.'],'Shares I do time. Think aloud: “Inside colour supports transport through internal pathways. A coloured outer surface alone would be weaker evidence.” Xylem name is helpful but not required for supported core explanation.',visual=insidewater),slide('We do',4,'Sequence the evidence before explaining',['B: setup.','C: stem colour later.','D: petal colour later still.'],'W1: B then C then D. Use evidence reader, sequencer and sceptic roles. Ask what the plain-water comparison adds: colour comes from the dye condition. Records are constructed; no universal 2-hour or 24-hour rate is implied.',table=table(['Stage','Dye shoot','Plain-water comparison'],[['Start','Placed in coloured water','Placed in plain water'],['Later','Internal red paths; then some red petals','No red dye colour']],'Constructed observations; timings vary in real demonstrations')),slide('We do',4,'Which claim reaches too far?',['“Water moved within this cut shoot.”','“We proved how roots took up water.”','Use B to choose.'],'W2: first supported; second unsupported because no roots. F also blocks “every missing drop entered the shoot”, because evaporation can reduce water level.',reveal='Explain the demonstrated part of the route and state what was not tested.'),slide('Hinge',3,'What does an internal red path support?',['Focus on evidence inside the shoot.'],'Correct first option. Revisit the section diagram if pupils treat the entire stem as one hollow pipe.',options=opts([('Coloured water moved through internal pathways.',True,'The tracer appears within the cut section.'),('The demonstration directly observed root absorption.',False,'There are no roots in this setup.'),('The dye is the plant’s natural food.',False,'It is a tracer; green plants make their own sugars.')]))]
d['interactive']=dict(title='Trace, test and limit the water claim',intro='Use the rooted model and constructed cut-shoot records. Reveal each diagram after a prediction; timings are not a live experiment.',slideIndex=5,steps=[step('In a rooted plant, which route fits soil water reaching a leaf?',[('Soil → roots → stem → leaves',True,'This follows connected water-carrying pathways.'),('Soil → petals → roots',False,'That reverses the main uptake route shown.')],wholewater,'Some water is retained or used; some leaves the plant as vapour.'),step('Colour appears inside an adult-cut stem section. Which account fits?',[('Internal water transport',True,'The tracer has reached pathways within the shoot.'),('Only paint on the outside',False,'The stated observation is inside the section.')],insidewater,'Compare the dye shoot with the plain-water shoot before claiming a dye effect.'),step('Can the rootless cut-shoot experiment demonstrate root absorption directly?',[('No',True,'The relevant roots are absent from the apparatus.'),('Yes',False,'Evidence from a rootless shoot cannot directly show what roots did.')],cutstem(),'It supports transport in the shoot above the cut; root uptake is explained with the separate rooted-plant model.')])
d['pupilPages']=[page('Shared evidence • The water-path case file','Records C–F are constructed teaching observations. Real dye timing varies; use the evidence supplied today.',[cards(watercards),resp('W1. Put setup, internal colour and petal colour in order.',2),resp('W2. Which part of the full rooted-plant route was not tested by B?',2)]),page('Route 1 • Build the water route','Word bank: soil / roots / stem / leaves / inside.',[resp('S1. Complete the route: soil → ___ → ___ → leaves.',2),choices('S2. The red paths in C are...',['Inside the stem section.','Only drawn on its outside.']),resp('S3. What was different about the water in the comparison shoot?',2),choices('S4. Did the cut shoots have roots?',['Yes','No']),resp('S5. State one thing the dye evidence supports.',2)]),page('Route 2 • Write a detective explanation','Use the observation and explain what it supports.',[resp('M1. Draw or describe the full water route in a rooted plant.',4),resp('M2. Explain how C and D support transport in the cut shoot.',3),resp('M3. Explain why E is a useful comparison.',2),resp('M4. State one thing B did not test and one reason F matters.',3)]),page('Route 3 • Challenge the overclaim','A report says: “All water travelled up the outside. We proved root uptake, and every drop missing from the cup entered the plant.”',[resp('T1. Use C to challenge the outside-only claim.',3),resp('T2. Use B to explain why direct root-uptake proof is missing.',3),resp('T3. Use F to explain an alternative cause of water loss.',3),resp('T4. Write a careful two-sentence conclusion that keeps the useful evidence.',4)])]
