# 8: controlled sliding run-out comparison, with no floor-sliding activity.
frictionforce=vis('Friction opposes sliding at the contact','A wooden block is already sliding to the right on a stationary horizontal surface after leaving a ramp. Friction on the block points left. The movement arrow is distinct from a force arrow; vertical forces are omitted from this simplified focus diagram.',[rect(65,350,865,65,'#DDE5EB',BLUE),rect(395,235,195,115,'#DECAB0',GOLD),line(410,155,775,155,GOLD),tx(430,90,410,55,'Sliding direction',30,True),line(445,330,185,330,BLUE),tx(65,245,305,65,'Friction on the block',27,True),tx(360,450,340,70,'Stationary surface',28,True),tx(40,510,930,35,'After release: no hand keeps pushing the block.',25)])
ramp=vis('Change the horizontal test surface only','Side-view fixed ramp feeds a sliding wooden block onto a horizontal test surface. The same block is released without a push from one marked point; ramp angle, release point, transition and block face stay fixed. Run-out is measured from the ramp end to the same chosen block edge when stopped.',[line(75,180,395,365,DARK,False,8),line(395,365,940,365,DARK,False,8),rect(400,370,540,40,'#DDE5EB',BLUE),rect(95,130,85,60,'#DECAB0',GOLD),tx(40,40,400,70,'Same ramp and release mark',29,True),line(140,120,140,160,GOLD,False,4),rect(745,305,85,60,'#DECAB0',GOLD),tx(580,170,330,95,'Block stops on\ntest surface.',28,True),line(395,435,745,435,BLUE,False,4),line(395,420,395,450,BLUE,False),line(745,420,745,450,BLUE,False),tx(460,460,360,55,'Run-out distance (cm)',27,True),tx(45,510,915,35,'Use the same trailing edge at ramp end and when stopped.',24)])
# The block is schematic at the release mark; no rotational projection is implied.
def frictionchart():
 e=[tx(40,30,920,55,'Constructed mean run-out distances',29,True),line(190,425,910,425,DARK,False,3),tx(280,495,600,45,'Run-out distance (cm)',27,True)]
 for value in [0,20,40,60]:
  xx=190+value*11;e +=[line(xx,120,xx,425,'#D6DEE4',False,2),tx(xx-30,435,65,40,str(value),25)]
 for yy,name,value,col in [(140,'Card',60,BLUE),(245,'Cloth',23,GREEN),(350,'Rubber',15,GOLD)]:
  e +=[tx(40,yy,130,45,name,28,True),rect(190,yy,value*11,55,col,col,1),tx(205+value*11,yy,85,50,str(value),28,True)]
 return vis('Compare distance after the same release','Horizontal bars start at zero and show constructed means: card 60 cm, cloth 23 cm, rubber 15 cm. They summarise three supplied repeated runs on each surface, not universal material constants.',e)
d=base('build_friction_surface_lab','The friction surface lab','Summer 2 • Choose a surface using controlled evidence',36,'Compare a sliding block’s movement on different surfaces and explain the effect of friction using a controlled test.',[('friction','A contact force that opposes sliding between surfaces.'),('surface','The outside layer in contact with an object.'),('run-out','Distance travelled along the level test surface after leaving the ramp.'),('fair comparison','A comparison with relevant conditions kept the same.')],['Describe friction opposing this block’s sliding.','Compare repeated run-out distances.','Name the changed surface and important controls.'],['A push or pull can change movement.','A comparison needs a consistent measurement rule.'],[('Friction is always useless.','Friction can provide useful grip or slow sliding; usefulness depends on the purpose.','Ask whether a surface should stop or allow the block to slide.'),('The surface with the longest run-out has the most slowing effect.','Under the same release conditions, greater resistance usually reduces run-out.','Compare card and rubber readings.'),('A smooth-looking material always has least friction.','Friction depends on the pair of materials and conditions; appearance alone is insufficient.','Ask for test evidence rather than a universal texture rule.')],[FRICTION])
d['equipment']+=['Optional low stable ramp with a smooth flush transition to a tabletop tray; small wooden sliding block; card, cloth and rubber test strips; ruler; barrier to catch the block.']
d['preparation']+=['Adult pilots a low ramp so the block slides without tumbling or snagging and stops within the tray. Fix the ramp and transition; use the same block/contact face and marked release point without a push.','The supplied constructed distances allow the full lesson without equipment. For real trials measure from the ramp end to the same trailing block edge at stopping; use three releases per surface and keep actual results separate.']
d['safety']=['Keep the activity on a stable table in a contained tray with a stop barrier. No pupils sliding on floors or ramps.','Use a small block, low ramp and clear release area; keep hands out of its path. No heavy falling objects or stretched elastic launchers.','Do not lubricate the surfaces or create a floor-slip hazard. Stop any setup that tips, snags or lets the block leave the tray.']
frrows=[['Card','58','60','62'],['Cloth','22','23','24'],['Rubber','14','15','16']]
d['slides']=[slide('Opening',2,'Which surface should stop the sliding block sooner?',['Predict from evidence.','Keep the release the same.'],'Set purpose: choose a surface for a small model stopping zone, not for real vehicle safety equipment.'),slide('Retrieval',3,'Retrieve pushes, pulls and comparisons',['1. What can a push change?','2. Name one thing to keep the same in a test.','3. What unit can measure a short distance?'],'1 motion/speed/direction. 2 relevant control accepted. 3 cm. Explain that a block already moving can continue after a hand stops pushing.'),slide('I do',7,'I distinguish motion from force',['The block slides right.','Friction on it acts left.','Contact with the surface matters.'],'Think aloud: “The direction of movement is not automatically the direction of every force. Here friction opposes the sliding and slows the unpushed block.” This is not a complete force diagram: weight and support act vertically.',visual=frictionforce),slide('I do',0,'I keep the start conditions fixed',['Same block, face and ramp.','Same release mark; no push.','Change only the level surface.'],'Shares I do time. Think aloud: “A higher release might travel further even on the same material. I keep the ramp and release fixed before comparing surfaces.” Fix surface strips flat with comparable transitions and no bunching.',visual=ramp),slide('I do',0,'I use repeats before choosing',['Compare all three runs.','A longer run-out means less slowing here.'],'Shares I do time. Think aloud: “Card is around 60 cm, rubber around 15 cm. In this setup rubber gives shorter run-out. I do not claim the material name gives the same result with every object.” Means are supplied summaries; core pupils can compare repeated ranges.',visual=frictionchart()),slide('We do',4,'The surface-selection meeting',['Read the three runs.','Choose the shorter stopping zone.','Use two readings in your reason.'],'W1: rubber gives shortest run-out (14–16 cm). Partners take client and evidence adviser roles; client challenges a claim with a table reading.',table=table(['Surface','Run 1 (cm)','Run 2 (cm)','Run 3 (cm)'],frrows,'Constructed sliding-block data; same ramp, block face and release')),slide('We do',4,'Repair a changed release',['A team pushes only the rubber run.','Can we compare it fairly?','Name the repair.'],'W2: no; a push changes initial conditions. Repeat using the same marked release without a push; retain/annotate the flawed run rather than silently replacing it.',reveal='Use comparable starts and record any method change.'),slide('Hinge',3,'The block slides right after release.',['Which friction statement fits this setup?'],'Correct first option. If second chosen, point to relative sliding at the contact.',options=opts([('Friction on the block acts left, opposing sliding.',True,'It opposes relative sliding on the stationary surface.'),('Friction acts right because the block moves right.',False,'Movement and friction point in opposite directions here.'),('No force can affect the block after the hand lets go.',False,'Contact friction can continue after release.')]))]
d['interactive']=dict(title='Replay the surface evidence',intro='Choose a surface and reveal the supplied run-out. This is a data replay, not a timed physical simulation. The paper table gives every run.',slideIndex=5,mode='friction',friction=dict(surfaces=[dict(name='Card',distanceCm=60,trials=[58,60,62]),dict(name='Cloth',distanceCm=23,trials=[22,23,24]),dict(name='Rubber',distanceCm=15,trials=[14,15,16])],note='Constructed means of three runs. Same wooden block/contact face, fixed ramp and flush transition, marked release without a push. Change only horizontal test surface. Distances describe this setup, not universal material constants.'),steps=[step('After release the block slides right. Which way does friction on it act?',[('Left',True,'Friction opposes its sliding over the stationary surface.'),('Right',False,'That confuses direction of motion with friction direction.')],frictionforce,'The hand need not remain in contact for friction to act.'),step('Which supplied surface gives the shortest run-out?',[('Rubber',True,'Its three readings are 14, 15 and 16 cm.'),('Card',False,'Card gives 58, 60 and 62 cm, the longest here.')],frictionchart(),'A shorter run-out fits the stopping-zone purpose in this model setup.'),step('A pupil adds a push only on one surface. What is the useful repair?',[('Repeat from the same mark without a push',True,'This restores comparable starting conditions.'),('Keep the push and call the comparison fair',False,'The starting motion now differs as well as the surface.')],ramp,'Record the flawed method honestly and label the new controlled trial.')])
d['pupilPages']=[page('Shared evidence • Constructed surface test','Same small wooden block and contact face; fixed low ramp and release mark; no push. Only the level test surface changes. Measure from ramp end to the same trailing block edge at stopping.',[tb(['Surface','Run 1 (cm)','Run 2 (cm)','Run 3 (cm)'],frrows),textblock('Mean run-out: card 60 cm; cloth 23 cm; rubber 15 cm. These are summaries of supplied teaching data, not universal properties of every material pair. Friction opposes this block’s sliding.'),resp('W1. Which surface best fits a short model stopping zone? Use evidence.',3),resp('W2. Why must we repair a run given an extra push?',2)]),page('Route 1 • Choose using the readings','Word bank: rubber / card / same / opposite / cm.',[choices('S1. The shortest run-out is on...',['Card','Cloth','Rubber']),resp('S2. Give one reading supporting your choice.',2),choices('S3. The block slides right. Friction on it acts...',['Right','Left']),resp('S4. Name two things the fair test keeps the same.',2),resp('S5. Would you give one run an extra push? Why?',2)]),page('Route 2 • Write a surface recommendation','Recommend a material for this model stopping zone, using repeated evidence.',[resp('M1. State the changed factor and measured outcome.',2),resp('M2. Compare card and rubber using their repeated ranges.',3),resp('M3. Explain friction’s direction and effect after release.',3),resp('M4. Write a short method with a consistent release, distance rule and repeat.',4)]),page('Route 3 • Review the test quality','A designer claims: “Rubber always has the most friction, so our result proves the safest surface for every purpose.”',[resp('T1. Limit the claim to the actual object and setup.',3),resp('T2. Explain why a different material pair or condition could need a new test.',3),resp('T3. A model needs a long sliding distance instead. Which supplied surface fits that purpose?',2),resp('T4. One cloth strip bunches up and stops the block. How should the team record and investigate this?',4)])]
end(d,['Read and select a surface.','Write an evidence-based recommendation.','Review the strength of the test.'],'Use supplied readings for the core task; optional practical after adult setup check. Keep actual values separately labelled. Do not require forces equations, coefficients or mean calculation for BUILD.','What made our surface choice defensible?','Comparable starts, repeated distances and a purpose-specific claim make the choice stronger.',['What does friction oppose in this test?','Which supplied surface gives the shortest run-out?','Name one start condition to keep the same.'],[('Retrieval 1–3','1 movement/speed/direction. 2 relevant controlled condition. 3 cm.'),('We do W1–W2','W1 rubber, 14–16 cm compared with card 58–62 cm and cloth 22–24 cm; it fits shorter run-out here. W2 an extra push changes starting conditions; repeat from the same release mark without a push and annotate the flawed run.'),('Hinge','First option correct. Friction on the right-sliding block points left; contact force persists after release.'),('S1–S5','S1 rubber. S2 14,15 or16 cm. S3 left. S4 same block/contact face, ramp/release point, transition, distance rule or other relevant condition. S5 no: changes start conditions.'),('M1–M4','M1 change horizontal surface; measure run-out distance in cm. M2 card 58–62 cm; rubber 14–16 cm, rubber shorter in every run. M3 friction opposes relative sliding, acts left on right-moving block and slows it after release. M4 fix ramp and mark; same block/face; release without push; measure from ramp end to same trailing edge at rest; repeat three runs per surface and retain records.'),('T1–T4','T1 rubber gave the shortest run-out for this wooden block/contact face and controlled setup. T2 friction depends on the pair, surface condition and loading; a different use needs relevant evidence. T3 card, about 60 cm here. T4 record the bunching/snag and affected run, secure the strip flat with comparable transition, repeat under the stated method and distinguish snagging from normal sliding resistance.'),('Exit E1–E3','E1 relative sliding between block and surface. E2 rubber. E3 fixed ramp angle/release mark, same block face, no push or comparable transition.')],['Horizontal run-out is a comparison of slowing in this apparatus, not a direct coefficient-of-friction measurement. Ramp losses and initial conditions must remain comparable.','Secure core: correct friction direction for this setup, table-supported comparison and one meaningful control. The lesson does not recommend surfaces for real safety-critical equipment.'])
